1
00:00:03,202 --> 00:00:07,305
This is Semantic Model Explainer, a Fabric App that draws

2
00:00:07,305 --> 00:00:10,472
a Power BI semantic model as a solar system.

3
00:00:10,922 --> 00:00:15,089
Fact tables are stars, dimensions are planets, measures are

4
00:00:15,089 --> 00:00:18,902
moons, and every relationship is a trail between them.

5
00:00:20,202 --> 00:00:23,911
The first lens is space weather: health findings show as

6
00:00:23,911 --> 00:00:27,952
flares on the tables, measures and relationships they affect.

7
00:00:28,751 --> 00:00:31,823
Today ranks what to fix first, and one click flies

8
00:00:31,823 --> 00:00:35,141
the camera to the relationship that filters both ways.

9
00:00:35,591 --> 00:00:39,341
The next item switches to the coverage lens: green moons

10
00:00:39,341 --> 00:00:43,091
are described and formatted, amber and red ones are not.

11
00:00:43,892 --> 00:00:46,956
The size lens counts rows with one batched query,

12
00:00:46,956 --> 00:00:49,832
so the biggest tables are the biggest planets.

13
00:00:50,632 --> 00:00:53,687
View as report author removes the hidden tables and

14
00:00:53,687 --> 00:00:57,342
measures, which is what people building reports actually see.

15
00:00:59,165 --> 00:01:02,294
Fly to any table or measure by name; the

16
00:01:02,294 --> 00:01:06,205
card shows its description, format string and DAX.

17
00:01:07,011 --> 00:01:10,443
The field guide lists every table and measure, with

18
00:01:10,443 --> 00:01:13,471
the DAX highlighted and one click to copy it.

19
00:01:13,921 --> 00:01:17,218
Filter for the measures nobody has described yet, or

20
00:01:17,218 --> 00:01:19,881
open a table and count its rows on demand.

21
00:01:20,688 --> 00:01:24,850
Space weather shows the eight rules and the score, with the

22
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:28,448
arithmetic on the page: 100 minus 40 minus 8 is 52.

23
00:01:29,252 --> 00:01:33,522
A forty-three table model becomes four star systems and a belt of

24
00:01:33,522 --> 00:01:38,252
loose tables, and a shared dimension sits between the stars it connects.

25
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:45,607
The star chart draws the same relationships flat, with arrows for filter

26
00:01:45,607 --> 00:01:49,760
direction, and it is the fallback when 3D is not available.

27
00:01:50,577 --> 00:01:53,193
The mission brief prints on one page, map

28
00:01:53,193 --> 00:01:55,937
included, or copies as Markdown for a wiki.

29
00:01:57,017 --> 00:01:59,575
Open a model, look at the sky, and you know

30
00:01:59,575 --> 00:02:01,717
what is in it and what to fix first.
